## Why This Is Asked
This tests cross-account policy design for SAP-to-SageMaker integration with JIT, MFA, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Tag-based principal mapping
- Least privilege IAM policies for SageMaker
- STS with MFA for ephemeral creds
- CloudTrail audit and GitOps IaC

## Code Example
```yaml
# Terraform-like snippet illustrating the cross-account role and trust
provider "aws" { region = "us-east-1" }
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_bridge" {
  name = "sap-sagemaker-bridge"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [{
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"Federated": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:saml-provider/sap"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
}
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "sap_bridge_policy" {
  role   = aws_iam_role.sap_bridge.id
  policy = jsonencode({
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [{
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["sagemaker:InvokeEndpoint"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:sagemaker:us-east-1:111122223333:endpoint/my-endpoint"
    }]
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you enforce per-SAP-object granularity if multiple SAP subsystems share a SageMaker endpoint?
- How would you test for drift and unauthorized access attempts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of cross‑account IAM with MFA, least privilege, and IaC integration in a real SAP on AWS scenario.

## Key Concepts

- STS assumeRole with MFA
- IAM trust policies and cross‑account access
- Least privilege for SAP HANA actions
- Terraform for IAM role and trust policy
- CloudTrail auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Terraform: IAM role with MFA‑enforced trust
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_operator" {
  name = "sap-operator"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = { AWS = "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:root" }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Condition = { aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent = "true" }
      }
    ]
  })
}
```

```javascript
// Terraform: least-privilege SAP actions
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "sap_operator_policy" {
  name = "sap-operator-policy"
  role = aws_iam_role.sap_operator.id
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Action = ["sap:Read", "sap:Execute"]
        Resource = "*"
      }
    ]
  })
}
```

```javascript
# CLI test: assume role with MFA and perform a harmless SAP action
aws sts assume-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/sap_operator --role-session-name sap-test \
  --serial-number arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:mfa/user --token-code 123456
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ongoing policy least-privilege using AWS Config or IAM Access Analyzer?
- What changes would you make to rotate credentials or rotate the operator role across regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, auditable cross-account access that bridges SAP authorizations with AWS IAM, while using JIT and MFA in a multi-region, multi-account setup. Tests integration points across IAM, SCPs, SSO, auditing, and basic IaC.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account trust and least privilege
- SCPs and IAM role mappings to SAP authorizations
- Just‑In‑Time elevation with AWS SSO and MFA
- Auditing: CloudTrail, Config, Central logging
- Minimal IaC for trust role and VPC endpoint service

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal Terraform-like snippet for cross-account trust role (pseudo)
provider "aws" { alias = "shared" }
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_operator_trust" {
  name = "sap-operator-cross-account"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow",
        Principal = { AWS = ["arn:aws:iam::111111111111:root"] },
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole",
        Condition = { StringEquals = { "sts:ExternalId" = "sap-access" } }
      }
    ]
  })
}

// Per‑BU session policy (inline for example)
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "sap_operator_policy" {
  name = "sap-operator-permission"
  role = aws_iam_role.sap_operator_trust.name
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["ec2:Describe*"], Resource = "*" },
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["sap:*"], Resource = "*" }
    ]
  })
}

// VPC Endpoint Service placeholder
resource "aws_vpc_endpoint_service" "sap_endpoints" {
  acceptance_required = true
  network_load_balancer_arns = [aws_lb.sap_nlb.arn]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the mapping between SAP authorizations and AWS roles at scale?
- How would you handle key rotation and revocation in this model?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-region SAP auth models, data residency, and auditable ops in a real-world multi-account setup.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-region SAP HANA access control with IAM/SCP mappings
- Just-In-Time (JIT) operator elevation with MFA
- PrivateLink for SAP component isolation across regions
- Centralized logging and compliance (CloudTrail, CloudWatch, Config)

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Resources": {
    "SAPCrossAccountRole": {
      "Type": "AWS::IAM::Role",
      "Properties": {
        "AssumeRolePolicyDocument": {
          "Version": "2012-10-17",
          "Statement": [
            {
              "Effect": "Allow",
              "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::[OPERATIONS_ACCOUNT]:root"},
              "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
              "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "true"}}
            }
          ]
        },
        "PermissionsBoundary": {"Fn::Join": ["", ["arn:aws:iam::", {"Ref": "AWS::AccountId"}, ":policy/SAPOperatorBoundary"]]},
        "Policies": [{
          "PolicyName": "SAPOperatorPolicy",
          "PolicyDocument": {
            "Version": "2012-10-17",
            "Statement": [{"Effect": "Allow", "Action": ["s3:ListBucket"], "Resource": "*"}]
          }
        }]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify and rotate SAP authorizations when roles drift?
- What monitoring would you implement to detect unauthorized JIT elevations?
